\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity. Using a matched-pair causal design on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 clause pairs spanning a six-type taxonomy evaluated across five models, we show that ambiguous clauses increase violations by 31.0 percentage points excluding incompleteness (34.0\,pp overall); cross-benchmark replication on SOPBench confirms directional consistency ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean; all five $\Delta > 0$, 3/5 significant at $p < 0.05$). However, ambiguity \emph{type} predicts \emph{how} agents fail, not \emph{whether} they fail: each type maps to a dominant failure mechanism with $\geq$3/5 cross-model consistency (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$, LLM-coded; human collapsed $\kappa = 0.41$; improved guidelines, prior round $0.15$). This \emph{type--mechanism dissociation} provides evidence that the taxonomy functions as a diagnostic instrument rather than a risk classifier. Error analysis reveals that agents almost never recognize ambiguity and converge on identical policy violations with strong permissive bias, showing that \citeauthor{berry_kamsties_2004}'s divergent-interpretation criterion does not extend to LLM agents. These findings, established under controlled conditions in two customer-service domains, motivate clause-level policy auditing over type-level risk screening.
\end{abstract}

