The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}); the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels, with type assigned post hoc. Using LLMs to code LLM failures risks circularity; however, type--mechanism mutual information (NMI $= 0.23$; Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[0.41, 0.50]$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this.\footnote{$V$ is computed on a $6\times 5$ table vs.\ $6\times 2$ for severity; higher dimensionality makes $V = 0.45$ conservative.} Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.32$, rising to $0.41$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary (improved guidelines; prior round $\kappa = 0.15$; Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}); we treat $V = 0.45$ as an upper bound pending larger-sample human validation. The higher inter-coder agreement among LLMs ($\kappa = 0.52$) than humans ($\kappa = 0.32$) may partly reflect shared classification conventions---a meta-level parallel to the convergent nocuity documented below---so the following patterns are best interpreted as categorization tendencies rather than ground-truth assignments. Two of six mappings reflect partial definitional affinity (authorization scope$\to$boundary violation, incompleteness$\to$gap-filling); the remaining four (e.g., scopal$\to$structural misparse, 82.7\% consistency) are empirically derived.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

Three failure patterns emerge ($\geq$3/5 cross-model consistency; Appendix~\ref{app:failure_details}): \emph{structural misparse}---incorrect parse commitment \citep{frazier_rayner_1982}---dominates scopal (5/5), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5); \emph{information gap-filling}---plausible but incorrect defaults---dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5); and \emph{boundary violation}---unauthorized escalation (69.2\%, $p < 10^{-4}$)---dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

