\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our ambiguity pairs are synthetically constructed, so the 34.0\,pp effect is an upper bound; the SOPBench replication ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean) provides a cross-benchmark lower bound. We evaluate seven API-accessible models across two customer-service domains at temperature $= 0$; other domains and smaller models remain untested. Failure mode coding relies on LLM annotators ($\kappa = 0.52$; human $\kappa = 0.32$, rising to $0.41$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary; improved guidelines, prior round $0.15$), though the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels, preventing type--mechanism contamination. All experiments use temperature $= 0$, measuring each model's modal interpretation---the policy-relevant quantity agents will most likely commit to and the typical deployment setting for policy-governed agents. The matched-pair design partially mitigates temperature effects: any variance increase at higher temperatures applies to both conditions, so the treatment effect direction is unlikely to reverse. Moreover, the unambiguous-condition violation rate is already near floor (6.9\%), so temperature-induced variance would predominantly affect the ambiguous condition, preserving or widening the gap. A temperature $= 0.7$ pilot on GPT-4.1 (Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}) confirms that the treatment effect is robust ($+$27.6\,pp vs.\ $+$28.0\,pp at temperature $= 0$; 97\% clause-level agreement), though tail-risk behavior under stochastic sampling across all models remains untested. Three additional scope limitations warrant disclosure: (1) three of the five judges share the OpenAI model family, which may inflate cross-model convergence estimates; (2) the coreferential type, whose variants differ only in referent specification, was excluded from both the mitigation and SOPBench replication analyses, limiting generalization for that type; and (3) the ICC analysis uses only within-family variation (GPT-4o/4o-mini), so the reported reliability bounds may not hold across architecturally diverse model pairs. Future work should extend to non-customer-service domains, scale the temperature pilot to all models, and collect per-episode human mechanism annotations to validate the type--mechanism association.

\section*{Data and Code Availability}
The 300 clause pairs, evaluation pipeline, analysis scripts, and raw episode traces will be released upon publication at \url{https://github.com/anonymous-nlp-2026/policy_ambiguity_safety}.

